<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tancred, King of Sicily</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1138–<a href="/years/1194">1194</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Tancred of Lecce</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tancred of Lecce was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from 1189 to 1194, an illegitimate grandson of Roger II. On the death of William II the Sicilian barons and officials chose him rather than Roger II's daughter Constance, wife of the future emperor Henry VI. He defended the kingdom successfully against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s first invasion, but after his death in 1194 Henry conquered Sicily.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tancred-king-of-sicily.jpg" alt="Tancred, King of Sicily" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tankred_syni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tancred was born in Lecce, the illegitimate son of Roger III, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, the eldest son of King Roger II, and of Emma, a daughter of Achard II, Count of Lecce. After Duke Roger's death, King Roger II kept Tancred and his brother William in close custody at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>. On 9 March 1161 Tancred joined his uncle Simon, Prince of Taranto, in invading the palace and detaining King William I and Queen Margaret.</p><p>The rising failed, and Tancred took pardon on condition of exile to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. He returned in 1166 under his cousin William II, was granted Montescaglioso before the end of 1168, and built it into the county of Lecce. He also served as constable and chief justiciar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> and the Terra di Lavoro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1174 Tancred led a Sicilian fleet to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, which briefly besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, and in June 1185 a fleet of 300 ships to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a>, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> was looted. When William II died in 1189, Tancred, though he had sworn fealty to Constance, seized the island and was crowned early in 1190. The vice chancellor Matthew d'Ajello and the official class backed him, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-iii">Pope Clement III</a> was persuaded to support him, while most nobles favoured Constance and her husband Henry VI. Critics mocked his short stature as that of &quot;The Monkey King&quot;.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> arrived in 1190 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, and demanded the release of his sister Joan, William II's widow, whom Tancred had imprisoned. Richard took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> on 4 October 1190, and in March 1191 Tancred agreed to a treaty, by which Joan was freed and Richard and Philip recognised Tancred as king. In 1191 Henry and Constance, crowned emperor and empress in April, invaded, but disease broke their army at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Tancred then held Constance captive, until Pope Celestine III secured her release in 1192 in exchange for recognition of Tancred as king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tancred won campaigns against the Apulian barons in 1192 and 1193. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> on 20 February 1194, two months after his son and co-king, Roger III. His widow Sibylla of Acerra governed for their young son William III. Henry VI returned that year with an army financed by the ransom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> surrendered in May. The people of Palermo admitted the emperor on 20 November 1194. Tancred's family fell into Henry's hands, and William III seems to have died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1198. Henry had Tancred's body taken from its grave. His death opened the way for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tancred of Sicily” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lecce, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.35,18.17&amp;year=1174" aria-label="Lecce on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4996 721 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tancred, King of Sicily"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5446 991h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5446 991h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.35,18.17&amp;year=1174">What was here in 1174? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
